  "summary": "The Chinese Bronze Age began in the Xia dynasty (c.",
  "description": "The Chinese Bronze Age began in the Xia dynasty (c. 2070 – c. 1600 BC) and reached its zenith during the Shang dynasty (c. 1600 – c. 1046 BC) and the early part of the Zhou dynasty (1045–256 BC). Its visual identity centers on ancient bronze ritual vessels and wares, marked by patina green tones, heavy metallic textures, symmetrical patterns, and dignified ritual vessel shapes. [ 1 ] [ 2 ]",
  "culturalContext": "First emerging in the Xia Dynasty (c. 2070–1600 BCE), simple bronze vessels, weapons and tools were made using early piece-mold casting. Nearly all major types of bronze vessels — ritual vessels, food vessels, wine vessels and the like — evolved directly from pottery prototypes of the Neolithic Age, resulting from material upgrading. The art reached its peak in the late Shang and early Western Zhou dynasties (c. 1600–771 BCE). Bronzes became large, skillfully made, and decorated. The technological breakthrough allowed bronze vessels to feature more complex shapes and more delicate decorative patterns. These decorative patterns on bronze ware can be traced back to the ornamentations on Neolithic pottery and jade artifacts, while also incorporating religious beliefs and symbols of power. In the mid-to-late Western Zhou and Spring and Autumn periods, styles turned simpler and more…",
